# Brief signal description — grounded and complete

## The rule

Every **`why_routing`** / **`signal_summary`** cell must let a manager understand **what happened** from that sentence alone — without opening the source URL.

This is **not** AI opinion. It is a **brief narrative** built from collect evidence, plus a routing tail.

## Required shape

```
{1–2 plain sentences: what page changed + what changed} → {routing matched to classification/tier}
```

**Opening pattern:** `We found a change on the {page}. {What changed — full sentences, stats in context.}`

**Do not prefix** `[Catch-up since last brief]` — if the row is in today's table, it is already implied.

**Only prefix when timing matters:**
- `[Weekend collect — Monday brief]`
- `[Page content changed since last brief]`
- `[Carryover spotlight]`

### Routing tail must match classification

| Classification | Tail must NOT say |
|----------------|-------------------|
| **Pricing** | "Compare to Core" — Pricing rows are Won't chase; no parity scan |
| **PMM / Talent / News** | "prototype" or "Core parity" unless manually promoted |
| **Product + Existing** | "needs parity scan" — parity is done |

### Must include (from collect data)

At least **one concrete fact** — embedded in prose, not bullet fragments:

1. Which page changed (`source_url`, `page_kind`, `source`)
2. What changed (snippet, entities, headline)

**Stats must have context.** Bad: `46% tour conversion when AI handles…` as a standalone fragment. Good: `LeaseHawk claims AI-handled prospects convert to tours at 46%, compared to 19% when staff handle tours without AI.`

### Must not be

- Stacked ` — ` fragments (page label — stat — packages — scrape note)
- A generic routing label alone
- Classification name with no substance
- Bare numbers without who/what comparison

### Good

> We found a change on the homepage. LeaseHawk claims AI-handled prospects convert to tours at 46%, compared to 19% when staff handle tours without AI (their marketing claim). No clear public rate card on the page. → Won't chase — competitor pricing/marketing packaging; monitor positioning only (no Core parity or prototype).

> We found a change on the pricing page. EliseAI updated published pricing to $2.50/unit/mo. → Won't chase — competitor pricing/marketing packaging; monitor positioning only (no Core parity or prototype).

> We found a change on the articles/blog index. New articles including "AI Is Changing the Way Your Renters Search" and "Fee Transparency: How All-In Pricing…". → Won't chase — PMM/positioning; no engineering PRD unless you promote manually.

### Bad

> homepage (pricing/packaging language) update — 46% tour conversion when their AI handles the prospect vs 19% when staff handle it — Packages/themes: ai, crm…

## Implementation

| Layer | Owner |
|-------|--------|
| Intel publish | `lib/briefSignalAnalysis.js` → `buildSignalAnalysis()` |
| Table rows | `lib/briefClassify.js` → `buildSignalsTableRows()` |
| Backfill | `scripts/reanalyze-signals-table.js --drop <id>` |

## The test

A manager reading only the Why column can answer: **What page? What changed? Why won't we chase it?**

